# Architecture Guidelines

## Project Structure

This is a Next.js 16+ application using the App Router with a separate FastAPI backend.

```
src/
├── app/                    # Next.js App Router
│   ├── (auth)/            # Auth route group (no dashboard layout)
│   ├── (dashboard)/       # Dashboard route group (with layout)
│   └── api/               # API proxy routes to FastAPI
├── components/
│   ├── ui/                # shadcn/ui base components
│   ├── features/          # Feature-specific components
│   ├── layouts/           # Layout components
│   └── shared/            # Reusable shared components
├── lib/
│   ├── api/               # API client and configuration
│   ├── hooks/             # Custom React hooks
│   │   ├── queries/       # TanStack Query hooks (GET)
│   │   ├── mutations/     # TanStack Mutation hooks (POST/PUT/DELETE)
│   │   └── common/        # Utility hooks
│   ├── utils/             # Utility functions
│   ├── schemas/           # Zod validation schemas
│   └── constants/         # App constants
└── types/
    ├── api/               # API response/request types
    └── models/            # Domain model types
```

## Key Architectural Principles

### 1. Separation of Concerns

- API layer handles all HTTP communication
- Hooks manage server state (queries/mutations)
- Components focus on UI and user interactions
- Utils contain pure, reusable functions

### 2. Backend Communication

- **Never call FastAPI directly from client components**
- Always use Next.js API routes (`/api/[...path]`) as a proxy
- Proxy hides backend endpoints for security
- Authentication via JWT in HTTP-only cookies

### 3. State Management Strategy

- **Server State**: TanStack Query (queries/mutations)
- **Client State**: useState, useReducer for component-level
- **Global Client State**: React Context when needed
- **NO Redux/Zustand for server state** - that's what TanStack Query is for

### 4. Type Safety

- TypeScript strict mode enabled
- Define types for all API responses/requests in `types/api/`
- Use Zod schemas for runtime validation
- Infer types from Zod schemas using `z.infer<typeof schema>`

### 5. Route Organization

- Use route groups: `(auth)` and `(dashboard)`
- Route groups don't affect URL structure
- Each route group can have its own layout
- Proxy handles authentication and redirects at the network boundary
